{
  "id": "Compound::DB00445 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Epirubicin is implicated in the pathogenesis of Breast Cancer through a complex network of interactions involving chemotherapy and inflammatory pathways. The evidence suggests that Epirubicin may contribute to the development of Breast Cancer via a chain of events that includes Caecitis and Injection Site Infection, both of which are treated by Paclitaxel and Capecitabine, respectively. This indicates a potential indirect relationship where Epirubicin could influence the progression of Breast Cancer through the actions of these chemotherapeutic agents. The core mechanism appears to involve the disruption of normal physiological processes, possibly through the modulation of inflammatory responses or the promotion of tumor growth, though the exact molecular pathways remain unclear. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of considering the broader context of drug interactions and their effects on both the disease and the body's response to treatment.",
